{"id": 1926345, "name": "RITTER v. ATLANTIC COAST LINE R. R. CO.", "name_abbreviation": "Ritter v. Atlantic Coast Line R. R.", "decision_date": "1909-07-19", "docket_number": "7249", "first_page": "213", "last_page": "215", "citations": [{"type": "official", "cite": "83 S.C. 213"}], "court": {"name_abbreviation": "S.C.", "id": 8894, "name": "Supreme Court of South Carolina"}, "jurisdiction": {"id": 59, "name_long": "South Carolina", "name": "S.C."}, "cites_to": [{"cite": "45 S. C., 496", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "case_ids": [368996], "opinion_index": -1, "case_paths": ["/sc/45/0496-01"]}, {"cite": "70 S. C., 180", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "opinion_index": -1}, {"cite": "62 S. C., 408", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "opinion_index": -1}, {"cite": "70 S. C., 216", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "pin_cites": [{"page": "555"}], "opinion_index": -1}, {"cite": "61 S. C., 73", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "opinion_index": -1}, {"cite": "73 S. C., 82", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "opinion_index": -1}, {"cite": "24 S. C., 132", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "case_ids": [4384083], "opinion_index": -1, "case_paths": ["/sc/24/0132-01"]}, {"cite": "20 S. C., 258", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "case_ids": [2341677], "opinion_index": -1, "case_paths": ["/sc/20/0258-01"]}, {"cite": "31 S. C., 517", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "opinion_index": -1}, {"cite": "26 S. C., 54", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "opinion_index": -1}, {"cite": "26 S. C., 64", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "opinion_index": -1}, {"cite": "20 S. C., 254", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "opinion_index": -1}, {"cite": "20 S. C., 255", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "opinion_index": -1}, {"cite": "4 Rich., 337", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "Rich.", "opinion_index": -1}, {"cite": "31 S. C., 152", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "opinion_index": -1}, {"cite": "82 S. C., 252", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "case_ids": [1924904], "opinion_index": 0, "case_paths": ["/sc/82/0252-01"]}, {"cite": "52 S. C., 323", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "case_ids": [378506], "opinion_index": 0, "case_paths": ["/sc/52/0323-01"]}], "analysis": {"cardinality": 215, "char_count": 3040, "ocr_confidence": 0.635, "pagerank": {"raw": 2.6016352678021274e-07, "percentile": 0.8195499042608275}, "sha256": "959aba9df7e99ebdbeb9b789a10025f97a774a23ae00165619e21304d1d74793", "simhash": "1:87b0f2480eba3405", "word_count": 521}, "last_updated": "2024-02-27T17:26:52.976801+00:00", "provenance": {"date_added": "2019-08-29", "source": "Harvard", "batch": "2018"}, "casebody": {"judges": [], "parties": ["RITTER v. ATLANTIC COAST LINE R. R. CO."], "opinions": [{"text": "The opinion of the Court was delivered by\nMr. Justice Gary.\nThis action was commenced before u magistrate to .recover damages for the killing of two cows belonging to the plaintiff.\nThe magistrate rendered judgment in favor of the plaintiff for $35, whereupon the defendant appealed to the Circuit Court. The appeal was dismissed, and the defendant has appealed again, upon several exceptions, which raise, in different form, the question presented by the first exception, which is as follows:\n\u201cThat the presiding Judge was in error in affirming the judgment of the magistrate, and in dismissing the appeal, because the presumption of negligence, arising from the mere fact that the stock was found dead on the defendant\u2019s right of way, was overcome by the uncontradicted testimony of the defendant to the effect that such killing was an unavoidable accident.\u201d\nThe killing of the stock by the defendant raised the presumption of negligence, which continued throughout the case, but subject to be overcome by the testimony of the defendant.\nIn the case of Mack v. Ry., 52 S. C., 323, the Court said: \u201cThe killing of the mule by the defendant raised the presumption of negligence on the part of the defendant, and this presumption continued until it was' rebutted by the testimony. The fact that the witnesses were examined by the plaintiff in regard to the killing of the mule did not destroy the presumption arising from the mere fact of killing, nor prevent the application of that rule. If the facts showed that there was no negligence, then, of course, the presumption would be ineffectual.\u201d\nA similar doctrine is announced in the case of Griffith v. Ry., 82 S. C., 252.\nUnder these decisions it can not be said that there was no evidence in favor of the plaintiff; and, as the evidence was conflicting, the findings of fact by the Circuit Court are not the subject of review by this Court.\nThe appeal is dismissed.", "type": "majority", "author": "Mr. Justice Gary."}], "attorneys": ["Mr. Jas. B. Peurifoy, for appellant,", "Messrs. Padgett & Lemacks, contra,"], "corrections": "", "head_matter": "7249\nRITTER v. ATLANTIC COAST LINE R. R. CO.\nLive Stock \u2014 Railroads\u2014Negligence\u2014Presumptions\u2014Appeal.\u2014Finding an animal dead on a railroad track raises a presumption of negligence on the part of the railroad company, which continues until it is rebutted by the evidence of the defendant. Where the evidence is conflicting in magistrate\u2019s court, this Court cannot consider whether the presumption has been overcome.\nBefore Memminger, J., Colleton, July, 1908.\nAffirmed.\nAction by A. R. Ritter against Atlantic Coast Line R. R. Co., in court of Magistrate J. E. Bryan. From order sustaining magistrate\u2019s judgment, defendant appeals.\nMr. Jas. B. Peurifoy, for appellant,\ncites: Plaintiff must show negligence: 31 S. C., 152. Artificial presumption is overcome by uncontradicted evidence: 4 Rich., 337; 20 S. C., 255; 20 S. C., 254; 26 S. C., 64.\nMessrs. Padgett & Lemacks, contra,\ncite: Presumption remains until overcome: 26 S. C., 54; 31 S. C., 517; 20 S. C., 258; 24 S. C., 132. This Court cannot review the facts: 73 S. C., 82; 61 S. C., 73; 70 S. C., 216, 555; 62 S. C., 408; 70 S. C., 180; 45 S. C., 496.\nJuly 19, 1909."}, "file_name": "0213-01", "first_page_order": 227, "last_page_order": 229}